WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.434
-That's a good entrance
right there.

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:03.868
-You've never run
the New York City Marathon?

00:00:03.868 --> 00:00:05.701
[ Laughter ]
I mean, there is a theme here.

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:08.033
-It surprises you?
Is that -- yeah.

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:09.200
Have you ever?
-I've run it three times.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.033
Thanks for asking.
[ Laughing ]

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:13.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Wow.

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:15.734
You did run it three times?
-No, I have to say,

00:00:15.734 --> 00:00:17.501
it is the best way
to see New York City.

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:19.334
Even if you, like,
run it really slowly,

00:00:19.334 --> 00:00:20.868
it doesn't matter.
It is amazing

00:00:20.868 --> 00:00:22.634
because every neighborhood
is different.

00:00:22.634 --> 00:00:24.901
All the crowds come out,
they -- they get you home.

00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:26.634
It's amazing.
It really is.

00:00:26.634 --> 00:00:28.133
-There's other ways
to see the city.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:29.234
-You're like,
"I'm still not doing it."

00:00:29.234 --> 00:00:30.534
[ Laughter ]

00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:32.567
-I'll watch on TV, yeah.
-Tour bus.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:33.934
-I've been out.
I've rooted people on.

00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:35.567
I probably rooted you on, yeah.

00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:37.701
-Yes, I'm sure you have.
-It is kind of amazing.

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:40.601
You know what's fun is
when we do the show on,

00:00:40.601 --> 00:00:42.400
I guess this Monday's show,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.334
a lot of audience will show up
with the medals.

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:45.801
-Of course.
-And it's pretty cool.

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:47.300
-But they don't take them off
for like three weeks.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.667
They got the medals on.
-No.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.434
That's when you unfollow
people on Instagram, too.

00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:51.901
You go, "I get it, you ran it.
I get it."

00:00:51.901 --> 00:00:54.534
-No, dude, 26.2,
like, you want some cred.

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:56.801
You're like, "I ran it."
-Yeah, no, I could never run

00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:58.133
even one mile.
Are you kidding me?

00:00:58.133 --> 00:00:59.701
I'm not in that good of shape.
But I will tell you --

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:01.667
By the way, it relates to you.

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:03.601
I'm a big fan of yours.
I've known you for a while.

00:01:03.601 --> 00:01:06.300
I have all of your cookbooks,
or a lot of them.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.334
-You don't have them all yet.
-How many do you have?

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.767
-I'm working on my 17th.
-No, I don't have 17.

00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.801
Is that right?
-How many do you think you have?

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:14.601
-Ballpark, I probably
have 6 or 7.

00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.167
-All right. So I'm gonna
get you the other 11.

00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:17.200
We're going to fill you in.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.667
-I would love that.
-Yes, absolutely.

00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:23.267
-But my dad got me a present
for Christmas, pretty recently,

00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:26.200
I want to say, of yours,
"Bobby Flay Fit."

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.367
-Oh.
-Which is good.

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:28.834
-He must have got that
on a discount table

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:30.334
because that's like
six years old now.

00:01:30.334 --> 00:01:32.400
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.100
-Maybe it was six years ago.
-"80% off! Here you go."

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.467
-No, but he got
this book for me.

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:39.534
But I also -- Kind of like
a -- like a backhanded gift.

00:01:39.534 --> 00:01:41.734
It's like, "Hey, here's --
It's called 'Get Fit.'"

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:43.267
-Oh, "Bobby Flay Fit," yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:45.300
My dad's like, "Hey, you're
looking a little doughy."

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.734
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:48.934
But anyways, so he got me this.
But it's a great cookbook.

00:01:48.934 --> 00:01:50.801
Great photos, by the way.
I do love your cookbook.

00:01:50.801 --> 00:01:52.067
I love your stories.

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:54.167
And I made a vegetarian chili
out of this book

00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:55.968
that is unbelievable.

00:01:55.968 --> 00:01:58.534
And I go, "Well, I don't know
if it's gonna be good

00:01:58.534 --> 00:01:59.667
without meat in it or whatever."

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:00.801
-Well, you're not a vegetarian,
are you?

00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:01.801
-No, I did it for fun.
-Oh, good,

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:02.934
'cause I'm cooking pork later.

00:02:02.934 --> 00:02:04.234
-Yeah.
-Just so you know.

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:05.868
-Yeah. No, I love it. Yeah.
-Yeah, I have to tell

00:02:05.868 --> 00:02:08.501
a quick story, 'cause I don't
even know if you remember this,

00:02:08.501 --> 00:02:12.033
but this is a -- this is
a Jimmy Fallon good-guy story.

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:13.901
-Oh, man.
-So -- you're like, "Uh-oh."

00:02:13.901 --> 00:02:16.200
No, no, no, really.
So, Giada De Laurentiis,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.267
who you've had
on the show before...

00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:18.501
-Yeah.
-...good friend of mine.

00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:21.167
She and I were gonna do
a daytime talk show.

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:23.634
And you know how, like,
you know, whenever --

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:25.567
whenever a new show
sort of, you know,

00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:28.234
starts to get a little bubble,
you have to do, like, pilots

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:30.801
and you have to -- you know,
see how it goes, so to speak.

00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:33.434
-Yes.
-And we use Rachael Ray's

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:34.801
audience one day as a surprise.

00:02:34.801 --> 00:02:37.167
They were all just like
you guys are here.

00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:39.801
And Giada and I came in,
and we took over the stage,

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:42.701
and we did our kind of bit
back and forth, you know?

00:02:42.701 --> 00:02:45.467
And you agreed --
You don't even remember this.

00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:50.501
You agreed to be our, like,
guest that we interviewed.

00:02:50.501 --> 00:02:51.834
It was never going
to air anywhere.

00:02:51.834 --> 00:02:53.133
And you just did, like --
you were just like,

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:54.734
"You know what?
I'm gonna do it for you.

00:02:54.734 --> 00:02:56.701
You've been on my show.
I'm gonna pay you back."

00:02:56.701 --> 00:02:59.667
And, like, you were amazing
as our -- as our guest.

00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:01.367
The crowd went crazy.

00:03:01.367 --> 00:03:03.801
I just want to let you know
the show never went anywhere.

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:05.934
[ Laughter ]
What happened was,

00:03:05.934 --> 00:03:09.100
they thought that Giada and I
had great chemistry.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.734
It was -- you know,
we're really killing it.

00:03:11.734 --> 00:03:14.801
They did say that the guest
segment was not compelling.

00:03:14.801 --> 00:03:16.367
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:18.567
-That's ridiculous.
That is ridiculous.

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:21.100
That is not true at all.
-It's not true.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.734
It's not true.
But I just want to --

00:03:22.734 --> 00:03:24.501
But, you know,
that was a bunch of years.

00:03:24.501 --> 00:03:26.901
And, you know, just thank you
for just being, like, you know,

00:03:26.901 --> 00:03:29.033
just part of it, you know?
You just -- it's so awesome.

00:03:29.033 --> 00:03:31.067
-You're a good man.
It's tough to book these things,

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:32.501
and you never know
what show is going to work

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:33.601
and not going to work.
-Of course not.

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:35.367
-But I'm a big fan
of you and Giada,

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:37.133
so, yeah, if you guys
are doing anything,

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:39.400
I'll show up to the party.
-Okay. You heard that, right?

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.434
Okay. Just want to make sure.
-Well, let's talk about

00:03:41.434 --> 00:03:43.200
Brasserie B.
-Oh, yes, Brasserie B.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.667
So Brasserie B
is my new restaurant

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:46.067
that I'm opening in Las Vegas.

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:47.234
I'm going to bring
a lot of different

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:48.701
of my own flavors to it.

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:50.167
I'm known for,
like, big flavors,

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:51.400
lots of chili peppers, you know.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:52.601
-But you went to culinary --

00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:54.901
-Yeah, I went to
the French Culinary Institute

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:57.868
in 1984.
-Is that right?

00:03:57.868 --> 00:03:59.834
-Yes, it is.
-And I think I have

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:02.033
a photo here.
Did you cook for Julia Child?

00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:04.467
-Yes, I cooked for Julia Child's
80th birthday.

00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:06.000
Amazing.
-Look at this.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.067
-I mean, I have to -- Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:11.267
-You're right there.
-Yeah. There I am.

00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:13.634
I'm one of these people here.
-That's pretty cool.

00:04:13.634 --> 00:04:15.400
-Well, Julia Child,
I have to say,

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.300
I was lucky enough
to spend a little time

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:18.834
with her
when she was alive,

00:04:18.834 --> 00:04:21.000
and she was
so incredibly gracious

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.434
to everybody
in the restaurants.

00:04:22.434 --> 00:04:24.501
The one thing about Julia
was she knew

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:26.968
in the best way
how important she was,

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:28.901
meaning she would
come into a restaurant,

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.767
and before she sat down,
she would go into the kitchen

00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:34.367
and shake everybody's hand
from the chef to the dishwasher.

00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:35.868
-Really?
-Yes, absolutely.

00:04:35.868 --> 00:04:36.934
And she was amazing.

00:04:36.934 --> 00:04:39.434
And Julia liked to have
a good time.

00:04:39.434 --> 00:04:43.033
I was lucky enough also to have
some lunches with her,

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:45.534
and she would sit down, and she
would call the server over,

00:04:45.534 --> 00:04:50.167
and she'd say, "I'm going to
have an upside-down martini,

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:53.033
and keep bringing them
until I tell you to stop."

00:04:53.033 --> 00:04:54.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.934
-What is an upside-down martini?
-That's what I said.

00:04:55.934 --> 00:04:57.367
I was like,
"What's an upside-down martini?"

00:04:57.367 --> 00:05:00.300
She said, "It's mostly vermouth
with a little bit of gin."

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.667
It's the opposite. Yeah.
-Wow!

00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:04.200
-She went for it. Julia.
-Wow!

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.033
-Lots of butter and lots of
upside-down martinis.

00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:09.033
She lived for a very long time.
-Yeah, she's a legend. Come on.

00:05:09.033 --> 00:05:10.300
-Absolutely.
-So you're opening up --

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.501
This is exciting.
So you get this brasserie,

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:15.234
and you're saying
your french fries...

00:05:15.234 --> 00:05:17.534
-Oh, yeah. Well, here's --
My thing is,

00:05:17.534 --> 00:05:18.901
I want to thank
the French right now

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:21.601
for giving us the best dish
of all time, French fries.

00:05:21.601 --> 00:05:23.267
Everybody wants french fries,
no matter what.

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:24.667
-Yeah.
Talk to me about this

00:05:24.667 --> 00:05:26.634
because I was asking you
about this backstage,

00:05:26.634 --> 00:05:29.601
and you actually said that
you built a whole separate room

00:05:29.601 --> 00:05:31.067
just for your fries...
-In the kitchen --

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:32.634
-...and that gets me excited.
-In the kitchen --

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:34.567
Here's the thing a lot of people
don't understand.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.634
In the kitchen --
Well, first of all,

00:05:36.634 --> 00:05:38.234
in my kitchen
that I'm building now,

00:05:38.234 --> 00:05:39.567
there's going to be
a room dedicated

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:41.033
just to making french fries.
-Yes.

00:05:41.033 --> 00:05:42.534
-And most people
don't understand this.

00:05:42.534 --> 00:05:44.701
It really takes two days
to make real french fries.

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:46.834
-What goes into making --
Because I think you go --

00:05:46.834 --> 00:05:48.267
-You want the process?
-Yeah.

00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:49.434
-Oh, you want the full process.
-I want to know.

00:05:49.434 --> 00:05:50.534
-Okay.
-We all want to know.

00:05:50.534 --> 00:05:52.367
Here's the way it works.
So you take, like,

00:05:52.367 --> 00:05:55.334
a russet or an Idaho potato,
you peel it.

00:05:55.334 --> 00:05:57.868
You cut it into
what looks like French fries.

00:05:57.868 --> 00:06:00.767
You throw it into cold water,
you let it soak overnight.

00:06:00.767 --> 00:06:02.834
You change the water a couple of
times, you get the starch out.

00:06:02.834 --> 00:06:05.200
-Well, already that's the step
I didn't know anything about.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:06.467
-Well, it takes a long --
Yeah.

00:06:06.467 --> 00:06:10.400
So you get the starch out.
Then you pat them dry,

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.167
and then you blanch them
at lower -- at a lower heat,

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:16.434
like maybe 300 degrees,
in some cooking oil.

00:06:16.434 --> 00:06:19.200
And you cook them
just until they're soft,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.234
but they're not brown
because it's not hot enough.

00:06:21.234 --> 00:06:23.801
So you're basically -- you're
tiring out the french fry.

00:06:23.801 --> 00:06:25.734
You're getting it
nice and soft, right?

00:06:25.734 --> 00:06:27.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:27.534 --> 00:06:29.968
Definitely edit that.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:29.968 --> 00:06:32.634
-So then --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:32.634 --> 00:06:34.033
Wait.
Where's the edit button?

00:06:34.033 --> 00:06:36.767
-No, no, no, you can't
control what we air.

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:38.367
All right.
So you make it real --

00:06:38.367 --> 00:06:39.868
you tire it and make it real
soft, right?

00:06:39.868 --> 00:06:41.067
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:44.000
So you soften your potatoes.
-Okay.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.033
Then you give it a couple --
give it a couple minutes,

00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:47.467
and then you hope
for the best.

00:06:47.467 --> 00:06:50.067
[ Laughter ]

00:06:50.067 --> 00:06:51.968
Hope for the best.
And you give it a couple --

00:06:51.968 --> 00:06:55.834
Let it rest for a half-hour
or something, yeah.

00:06:55.834 --> 00:06:57.934
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.934 --> 00:06:59.934
-You're a better man than me.
-Then you try it again,

00:06:59.934 --> 00:07:01.601
and then you try it again.

00:07:01.601 --> 00:07:04.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:04.601 --> 00:07:06.267
All right.
You take a blue pill,

00:07:06.267 --> 00:07:08.367
and then you go,
"Yeah, here we go.

00:07:08.367 --> 00:07:10.868
Let's try this again.
Let's see what's happening."

00:07:10.868 --> 00:07:13.968
And then you make it.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.968 --> 00:07:16.367
-Then you turn up
the heat to 375.

00:07:16.367 --> 00:07:18.868
-Oh, yeah, get it hot.
-And then you brown them.

00:07:18.868 --> 00:07:20.968
-Yeah.
-And they're crispy,

00:07:20.968 --> 00:07:22.801
and they're soft and delicious.
-Yeah.

00:07:22.801 --> 00:07:24.367
-And then that's it.
-Yeah. So --

00:07:24.367 --> 00:07:25.801
-French fries.
-That's french fries.

00:07:25.801 --> 00:07:27.234
That's french fries.
-French fries.

00:07:27.234 --> 00:07:28.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.634
-Ooh la la.
That was the sexiest...

00:07:30.634 --> 00:07:33.067
That's the sexiest description
of french fries

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:35.167
that's ever been done.
Can we talk about

00:07:35.167 --> 00:07:36.734
"Beat Bobby Flay"?
-Yes, we can.

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:39.100
-"Holiday Throwdown."
I don't know --

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.300
I've never been on
"Beat Bobby Flay."

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:41.467
You know why I wouldn't go on?

00:07:41.467 --> 00:07:44.234
Because I don't want
to ever beat you.

00:07:44.234 --> 00:07:45.334
I want to be friends with you.

00:07:45.334 --> 00:07:46.534
-I really want you
to come on the show.

00:07:46.534 --> 00:07:47.801
-No, you don't.
-Yes, I do, Jimmy.

00:07:47.801 --> 00:07:49.734
-No, I only go on --
If you have a talk show,

00:07:49.734 --> 00:07:51.834
I'm on it.
I don't want to --

00:07:51.834 --> 00:07:53.100
-You limit your scope?
-Yeah.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:54.367
[ Laughter ]

00:07:54.367 --> 00:07:57.267
But I think --
I want to see you win.

00:07:57.267 --> 00:07:59.267
-Eh.
-I like it when you don't win.

00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:02.467
But, you know, why --
-I created the show to get beat,

00:08:02.467 --> 00:08:04.601
so I'm okay with it.
-I once watched a marathon

00:08:04.601 --> 00:08:08.167
of you for...
-Eating your vegetarian chili.

00:08:08.167 --> 00:08:10.367
-...30 hours of, like,
"Beat Bobby Flay."

00:08:10.367 --> 00:08:12.167
It was on Food Network.
-Really?

00:08:12.167 --> 00:08:13.567
-It was --
It was a three-day event,

00:08:13.567 --> 00:08:15.467
and I watched every one.
-So you do do marathons?

00:08:15.467 --> 00:08:17.868
-Oh, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.868 --> 00:08:19.634
Come on! That's a great joke!

00:08:19.634 --> 00:08:22.267
[ Cheers and applause ]
This guy's just unbelievable.

00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:25.100
Come on! That's a fantastic --
That's a great joke.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.634
-What do you call that, recall?
-That's a -- no, callback.

00:08:27.634 --> 00:08:29.400
-Callback, callback.
-Yeah, that's great.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.267
But anyways,
this "Holiday Throwdown,"

00:08:32.267 --> 00:08:34.567
you're taking on other
Food Network stars.

00:08:34.567 --> 00:08:35.934
-Well, yes.
And the great thing

00:08:35.934 --> 00:08:38.667
about the the holiday
version of "Beat Bobby Flay" is,

00:08:38.667 --> 00:08:40.300
it's all
the Food Network stars

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:42.000
that people know
who watch the network.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.834
And they all think that
we kind of hang out together

00:08:43.834 --> 00:08:45.133
and live together anyway,

00:08:45.133 --> 00:08:48.067
which of course we do.
-Yeah, live in one building

00:08:48.067 --> 00:08:49.968
like The Monkees.
-Exactly.

00:08:49.968 --> 00:08:51.000
Bunk beds.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.534
-And -- But it is really,
really fun.

00:08:53.534 --> 00:08:55.601
It's a great environment,
great atmosphere.

00:08:55.601 --> 00:08:57.734
It's rated so well the first
couple of years we do it,

00:08:57.734 --> 00:08:59.167
so people just
keep watching it.

00:08:59.167 --> 00:09:00.467
Yeah.
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:09:00.467 --> 00:09:01.501
Here's a sneak peek at

00:09:01.501 --> 00:09:03.033
"Beat Bobby Flay:
Holiday Throwdown."

00:09:03.033 --> 00:09:04.934
Take a look at this.

00:09:04.934 --> 00:09:06.534
-Bobby.
-What?

00:09:06.534 --> 00:09:08.834
-What's your take
on the turkey tetrazzini?

00:09:08.834 --> 00:09:11.634
-Well, this is like
an old family recipe of mine.

00:09:11.634 --> 00:09:14.467
So I'm gonna use
turkey sausage

00:09:14.467 --> 00:09:16.934
that I'm spicing up
with some Calabrian chili.

00:09:16.934 --> 00:09:18.167
Egg noodles.

00:09:18.167 --> 00:09:20.367
I'm going to make
a fontina béchamel

00:09:20.367 --> 00:09:21.934
to kind of layer
it all together.

00:09:21.934 --> 00:09:24.501
-Wait. So you're not
actually cooking a turkey?

00:09:24.501 --> 00:09:26.167
You're using turkey sausage?

00:09:26.167 --> 00:09:28.934
-Yeah. Well, there's turkey
and turkey sausage, Katie.

00:09:28.934 --> 00:09:30.300
[ Laughter ]

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.534
-You're burning each other.

00:09:31.534 --> 00:09:33.868
Why not?
Why not? Have fun.

00:09:33.868 --> 00:09:35.467
-It's like, "Who's buried
in Grant's tomb?"

00:09:35.467 --> 00:09:38.434
-Exactly. "Beat Bobby Flay:
Holiday Throwdown"

00:09:38.434 --> 00:09:40.634
premieres Tuesday
on Food Network.

00:09:40.634 --> 00:09:43.067
We're cooking a dish
from Bobby's new restaurant

00:09:43.067 --> 00:09:44.167
